Cancel is not a choice
====================

`pick_folder` on Linux returned a path even when nothing was chosen: if the platform picker came back
empty — no dialog available, or the person pressed Cancel — it fell through to a substituted default and
handed that back. The caller could not tell the difference between "they picked this" and "they
declined", so cancelling quietly set the model folder to the workspace.

It returns `None` now, which is what an empty answer means. The macOS path is unchanged.

diff -Naur a/crates/quixi-platform/src/lib.rs b/crates/quixi-platform/src/lib.rs
--- a73/crates/quixi-platform/src/lib.rs	2026-11-02 15:47:20.632761052 -0800
+++ b73/crates/quixi-platform/src/lib.rs	2026-11-02 15:47:20.694079322 -0800
@@ -919,10 +919,14 @@
         }
 
         #[cfg(target_os = "linux")]
-        if let Some(picked) = crate::linux::pick_folder(prompt) {
-            return Some(picked);
+        {
+            // None means "nothing was chosen" — either the desktop had no dialog to offer or the
+            // person cancelled. Both used to fall through to a substituted default, so pressing
+            // Cancel quietly picked the workspace folder as though it had been asked for. A cancel
+            // that returns a path is a lie the caller cannot detect.
+            return crate::linux::pick_folder(prompt);
         }
-        #[cfg(not(target_os = "macos"))]
+        #[cfg(not(any(target_os = "macos", target_os = "linux")))]
         {
             let _ = prompt;
             Some(quixi_proto::paths::default_workspace_str().to_string())
